# ⛔ ONE LINE, AND ITS NARROWNESS IS THE POINT. Read this before adding a second path.
#
# WHAT IT BUYS. Every PR that ships a card appends its entry at the SAME anchor — the top of
# `[Unreleased]` in docs/CHANGELOG.md (VERSIONING.md § The `[Unreleased]` entry rule, gated by
# tests/changelog-card-entry-selftest.sh) — so every merge to `dev` leaves every open sibling
# branch colliding there. Measured at five hand-resolutions in one session, and superlinear in
# parallelism: N open PRs mean each merge dirties N-1. With this line, the `git merge origin/dev`
# a sibling branch runs to catch up resolves that collision by keeping both entries instead of
# stopping for a hand-edit. That is the benefit, and it is LOCAL — it removes the hand-edit, not
# the catch-up merge.
#
# ⛔ IT DOES NOT UN-DIRTY A PULL REQUEST, AND THAT WAS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. GitHub's
# server-side merge does not apply this driver. A throwaway branch pair colliding only on
# `[Unreleased]`, both carrying this file and sharing a merge base that carries it, was refused
# by `POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/merges` with `409 Merge conflict` — the SAME answer as the
# no-attribute control pair, while a pair touching different files answered `201` (so the
# endpoint does discriminate) and a local `git merge-tree` on that very union pair answered rc 0.
# Measured 2026-08-22 against this repo. So a sibling PR still goes `CONFLICTING`, and still gets
# no `pull_request` workflow run — a check set of `total_count: 0`, which is neither green nor
# red and which a naive "any failures?" read answers *no*. ⇒ Never cite this line as CI
# protection; the only fix for a dirty PR is still to merge `dev` into the branch (card#7227).
#
# ⛔ DO NOT WIDEN THIS TO ANOTHER PATH BY SYMMETRY. `union` concatenates lines with no idea what
# they mean, and two shapes were measured being DESTROYED by it. NEITHER FILE IS IN THIS REPO —
# `git ls-files` carries neither — so a maintainer who comes here for the precedent before
# widening must look where they live:
#   * an append-only log whose entries carry a NUMERIC key — `CLAUDE_DECISIONS.md`, a file of the
#     kanban-board and agent-webhook-bridge repos, not of this one. Union interleaves by
#     position, silently producing the wrong order.
#   * one long table row edited on BOTH sides — `docs/config-schema.md` in agent-webhook-bridge.
#     Union emits the row TWICE, and picking a side drops whichever change lost. That one needed
#     both semantic additions spliced into one row by hand; no automatic rule could have produced
#     it.
#
# ⚠ AND THE GROUND FOR INCLUDING THE CHANGELOG IS NOT "IT IS ONLY EVER APPENDED TO". That was
# the original argument and it is FALSE: entries here are reworded in place, including this
# file's own entry and entries in the sibling repos that run the same rule. The distinction that
# survives is
# narrower and is a trade, not a clean separation. On the changelog the collision that RECURS is
# two PRs appending at one anchor, where "keep both, in either order" is the right answer and
# union produces it; the in-place edit is the rare case, and it is disclosed below with an
# operating rule rather than gated. On a table row there is no append case at all — every
# collision is an in-place edit of one line, so union is wrong every time, with nothing on the
# other side of the trade. Union is right for the common case here and wrong for the rare one;
# there it is wrong for the only case.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT CHANGE. Every other path merges exactly as it did before, and a conflict in
# any of them still stops for a human. Nothing about the CHANGELOG's content rules moves either:
# the entry is still per-PR and still gated.
#
# RESIDUALS, each measured on this file's real structure against its own no-attribute control.
# One is gated; the rest are operating rules:
#   * UNION IS SILENT, and it applies to every three-way merge git performs — `merge`, `rebase`,
#     `cherry-pick` and `revert` — not only to a PR merge.
#       - REBASE is the one that bites hardest. A branch that REWORDS its own entry and is then
#         rebased onto a `dev` that gained a sibling entry at the same anchor keeps BOTH
#         wordings: rc 0, "Successfully rebased and updated refs/heads/…", nothing printed
#         (control: `CONFLICT (content)`, rc 1 — the rebase stops for a human). The rebased
#         commit records no removal at all, so the branch's history reads as two entries added.
#         ⇒ NEVER REBASE A BRANCH THAT HAS REWORDED ITS OWN `[Unreleased]` ENTRY. Merge `dev`
#         into it instead. GATED, for line-initial bullets only:
#         tests/changelog-card-entry-selftest.sh reds on any card for which ONE PR left two
#         bullets standing. Its population is BULLETS, not entry bodies — a duplicated line
#         inside a multi-line entry is invisible to it.
#       - REWORD AGAINST REWORD, in an ordinary merge: two branches that each edit the SAME
#         existing `[Unreleased]` line emit BOTH spellings at rc 0 — a changelog that
#         contradicts itself, with no conflict and no prompt (control: `CONFLICT (content)`,
#         rc 1). NOT gated, and not gateable from the merged tree: the surviving pair is
#         indistinguishable from two PRs that each filed their own entry for one card, which
#         this file legitimately contains twice (card#6645, card#7038). ⇒ A PR THAT EDITS AN
#         EXISTING ENTRY RATHER THAN APPENDING ONE MUST READ THE MERGE COMMIT'S DIFF OF
#         `[Unreleased]`.
#       - CHERRY-PICK: picking a commit that rewords an existing entry onto a branch that
#         appended a new one leaves both spellings, stale copy included (control: it conflicts).
#       - REVERT of a commit whose entry sits beside a later one becomes a NO-OP — `nothing to
#         commit, working tree clean`, rc 1, entry still in the file — because union re-adds
#         exactly what the reverse patch removes (control: a conflict, ALSO rc 1, so the exit
#         code does not distinguish them; read the message).
#     A merged `[Unreleased]` is never presented for review, so read the merge commit's diff of
#     it: a clean merge is not a reviewed one.
#   * ORDER FOLLOWS MERGE DIRECTION, and one direction files an entry in the wrong section. With
#     a release rename (`[Unreleased]` → `[X.Y.Z]`) as OURS and a feature branch as THEIRS, the
#     incoming entry lands UNDER the version heading — well-formed, clean, and wrong; the
#     opposite direction is correct. So: merge `dev` INTO the feature branch, and never merge a
#     feature branch into `release/v<version>` — re-cut the release branch instead.
docs/CHANGELOG.md merge=union
